1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,054
Dawn, September 15th, 1950.

2
00:00:03,074 --> 00:00:05,829
The sea off Inchon, Korea.

3
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:07,959
In the darkness, more than two hundred

4
00:00:07,979 --> 00:00:10,248
and sixty ships lay waiting in silence,

5
00:00:10,268 --> 00:00:12,273
their crews holding their breath.

6
00:00:12,613 --> 00:00:15,591
But this was a strange and treacherous sea.

7
00:00:15,611 --> 00:00:18,528
A sea that vanished twice a day.

8
00:00:18,568 --> 00:00:19,971
When the tide went out,

9
00:00:19,991 --> 00:00:22,326
up to four kilometers of mudflats lay

10
00:00:22,346 --> 00:00:24,540
bare in front of the shore.

11
00:00:25,021 --> 00:00:26,663
Ships could reach the shore only for

12
00:00:26,683 --> 00:00:29,200
two or three hours around high tide.

13
00:00:29,299 --> 00:00:30,918
Miss that window, and the landing

14
00:00:30,938 --> 00:00:33,136
force would be stranded on the mud.

15
00:00:33,493 --> 00:00:35,389
The man who pushed for this operation:

16
00:00:35,409 --> 00:00:37,881
Douglas MacArthur, Commander-in-Chief

17
00:00:37,901 --> 00:00:39,917
of the United Nations Command.

18
00:00:40,086 --> 00:00:42,309
It was a gamble so risky it came to be

19
00:00:42,329 --> 00:00:45,097
called a five-thousand-to-one shot.

20
00:00:45,587 --> 00:00:48,227
And the gamble paid off in a single day.

21
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:51,421
Yet something strange followed. From Inchon

22
00:00:51,441 --> 00:00:54,823
to Seoul was barely thirty kilometers.

23
00:00:54,888 --> 00:00:56,370
And retaking Seoul would

24
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:58,779
take thirteen full days.

25
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,772
For the Marines who crossed the sea,

26
00:01:00,792 --> 00:01:02,936
the real hell was waiting in Seoul.

27
00:01:03,292 --> 00:01:04,885
The Inchon Landing and

28
00:01:04,905 --> 00:01:06,671
the Recapture of Seoul.

29
00:01:06,699 --> 00:01:07,738
This is the story of a

30
00:01:07,758 --> 00:01:09,638
five-thousand-to-one gamble,

31
00:01:09,658 --> 00:01:12,799
and thirteen days of street fighting.

32
00:01:13,136 --> 00:01:16,529
June 25th, 1950. A surprise North

33
00:01:16,549 --> 00:01:18,724
Korean invasion began the war.

34
00:01:18,894 --> 00:01:21,640
Seoul fell in just three days, and South

35
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:24,462
Korean and American troops were pushed south,

36
00:01:24,482 --> 00:01:27,409
and further south, week after week.

37
00:01:27,442 --> 00:01:29,140
By August, all that remained was a

38
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:32,009
narrow corner east of the Naktong River.

39
00:01:32,361 --> 00:01:35,030
This last line, with its back to Pusan,

40
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:37,489
was called the Pusan Perimeter.

41
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:40,073
By late August, North Korea was preparing

42
00:01:40,093 --> 00:01:42,623
a final all-out offensive to break it.

43
00:01:42,742 --> 00:01:44,930
One more breach, and South Korea

44
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:46,984
would be pushed into the sea.

45
00:01:47,333 --> 00:01:49,674
On September 1st, North Korea struck

46
00:01:49,694 --> 00:01:52,416
along the entire Naktong front.

47
00:01:52,532 --> 00:01:55,289
From Yongsan to Tabu-dong, from Yongchon

48
00:01:55,309 --> 00:01:58,214
to Pohang, the line began to crack.

49
00:01:58,310 --> 00:01:59,963
The defenders survived only by

50
00:01:59,983 --> 00:02:02,036
rushing their last reserves from one

51
00:02:02,056 --> 00:02:04,602
breach to the next, day and night.

52
00:02:04,926 --> 00:02:07,615
Meanwhile, at UN headquarters in Tokyo,

53
00:02:07,635 --> 00:02:10,266
a very different plan was taking shape.

54
00:02:10,423 --> 00:02:12,430
Land a massive force far behind

55
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:14,217
enemy lines, at the port of

56
00:02:14,237 --> 00:02:16,856
Inchon, the gateway to Seoul.

57
00:02:16,976 --> 00:02:19,806
Its name was Operation Chromite.

58
00:02:20,128 --> 00:02:22,043
MacArthur reportedly first imagined

59
00:02:22,063 --> 00:02:24,933
this plan just four days into the war.

60
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:26,983
Watching Seoul burn from a hill

61
00:02:27,003 --> 00:02:28,839
south of the Han, he pictured

62
00:02:28,859 --> 00:02:30,614
a blow to the enemy's rear.

63
00:02:30,693 --> 00:02:32,732
A landing planned in July had to

64
00:02:32,752 --> 00:02:34,633
be shelved for lack of troops,

65
00:02:34,653 --> 00:02:36,749
but he never let go of Inchon.

66
00:02:37,110 --> 00:02:38,418
The idea was simple.

67
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:39,832
Every North Korean supply

68
00:02:39,852 --> 00:02:42,040
line ran south through Seoul.

69
00:02:42,129 --> 00:02:45,000
Cut that lifeline, and the entire enemy army

70
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:47,140
fighting at the Naktong would be trapped,

71
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:50,753
with no food, no ammunition, and no way home.

72
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:53,568
But Washington's response was cold.

73
00:02:53,707 --> 00:02:56,292
On August 23rd, the Army and Navy chiefs

74
00:02:56,312 --> 00:02:59,206
of staff flew to Tokyo for a conference.

75
00:02:59,374 --> 00:03:01,597
One by one, Navy officers reported that

76
00:03:01,617 --> 00:03:04,374
Inchon had nearly every natural condition

77
00:03:04,394 --> 00:03:06,895
that makes an amphibious landing fail.

78
00:03:07,249 --> 00:03:09,836
Tides, mudflats, a narrow channel,

79
00:03:09,856 --> 00:03:12,635
a fortress island, and high seawalls.

80
00:03:12,812 --> 00:03:15,102
Some even proposed a safer landing farther

81
00:03:15,122 --> 00:03:18,297
south, on the open beaches near Kunsan.

82
00:03:18,638 --> 00:03:19,796
MacArthur answered with a

83
00:03:19,816 --> 00:03:21,754
long, passionate speech.

84
00:03:21,824 --> 00:03:23,648
Because everyone believed a landing

85
00:03:23,668 --> 00:03:25,720
there was impossible, he argued,

86
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:28,155
the enemy would never be ready for it.

87
00:03:28,204 --> 00:03:29,823
The room fell silent,

88
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:32,496
and the operation was approved.

89
00:03:32,682 --> 00:03:34,756
D-Day was September 15th.

90
00:03:34,776 --> 00:03:36,991
Barely three weeks away.

91
00:03:37,321 --> 00:03:39,417
Why was Inchon so dangerous?

92
00:03:39,437 --> 00:03:41,824
The map tells the story.

93
00:03:41,845 --> 00:03:44,215
From the open sea to Inchon harbor,

94
00:03:44,235 --> 00:03:45,706
there was only one narrow

95
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:48,324
channel between islands and shoals.

96
00:03:48,424 --> 00:03:51,456
Its name: Flying Fish Channel.

97
00:03:51,791 --> 00:03:53,849
Currents reached up to eight knots,

98
00:03:53,869 --> 00:03:56,364
nearly fifteen kilometers an hour.

99
00:03:56,481 --> 00:03:58,613
One sunken ship or a single mine in

100
00:03:58,633 --> 00:04:00,332
that channel could leave the whole

101
00:04:00,352 --> 00:04:03,607
fleet behind it stranded and helpless.

102
00:04:03,946 --> 00:04:06,475
But the greatest enemy was the sea itself.

103
00:04:06,495 --> 00:04:09,373
The tidal range reached about nine meters.

104
00:04:09,462 --> 00:04:11,012
That's like the height of a three-story

105
00:04:11,032 --> 00:04:13,778
building, rising and falling twice a day.

106
00:04:13,868 --> 00:04:16,807
At low tide, landing craft stuck fast

107
00:04:16,827 --> 00:04:18,810
in the mud, and the men inside

108
00:04:18,830 --> 00:04:21,553
became easy targets on open ground.

109
00:04:21,886 --> 00:04:24,302
Only a few days a month brought tides

110
00:04:24,322 --> 00:04:26,566
high enough to reach the seawalls.

111
00:04:26,713 --> 00:04:28,605
Miss September 15th, and the

112
00:04:28,625 --> 00:04:30,541
next chance came in mid-October,

113
00:04:30,561 --> 00:04:32,382
when the cold was setting in.

114
00:04:32,733 --> 00:04:34,124
And right in front of the harbor

115
00:04:34,144 --> 00:04:37,197
stood a fortress island: Wolmi-do.

116
00:04:37,245 --> 00:04:39,172
Unless Wolmi-do fell first,

117
00:04:39,192 --> 00:04:41,323
the main force would expose its flank

118
00:04:41,343 --> 00:04:43,040
the moment it entered the port.

119
00:04:43,369 --> 00:04:45,188
But between the attack on Wolmi-do

120
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:47,522
and the main landing, the ebb tide

121
00:04:47,542 --> 00:04:50,013
left a gap of nearly twelve hours.

122
00:04:50,099 --> 00:04:51,605
Wolmi-do would be taken on the

123
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:53,733
morning tide, and the main force would

124
00:04:53,753 --> 00:04:56,271
land only on the evening tide.

125
00:04:56,347 --> 00:04:58,456
That was more than enough time for the enemy

126
00:04:58,476 --> 00:05:00,448
to guess exactly where the main landing

127
00:05:00,468 --> 00:05:03,648
would come, and to rush in reinforcements.

128
00:05:03,993 --> 00:05:06,755
Deceiving the enemy mattered just as much.

129
00:05:06,923 --> 00:05:09,828
UN forces bombed and shelled Kunsan and

130
00:05:09,848 --> 00:05:13,256
the east coast, faking landings elsewhere.

131
00:05:13,276 --> 00:05:14,895
Two days before the landing,

132
00:05:14,915 --> 00:05:16,853
destroyers sailed right up to

133
00:05:16,873 --> 00:05:19,368
Wolmi-do to draw the enemy's fire.

134
00:05:19,395 --> 00:05:21,231
The moment those guns fired back,

135
00:05:21,251 --> 00:05:23,769
their carefully hidden positions were revealed,

136
00:05:23,789 --> 00:05:26,276
and the warships destroyed them.

137
00:05:26,615 --> 00:05:28,692
So before the landing, someone had

138
00:05:28,712 --> 00:05:30,866
to go into those waters first.

139
00:05:30,972 --> 00:05:33,456
In mid-August, South Korean naval

140
00:05:33,476 --> 00:05:36,222
intelligence agents, disguised as fishermen,

141
00:05:36,242 --> 00:05:38,862
slipped onto Yonghung-do island.

142
00:05:39,037 --> 00:05:42,603
Water depth, mud firmness, gun positions,

143
00:05:42,623 --> 00:05:46,192
seawall heights. They measured it all by hand.

144
00:05:46,504 --> 00:05:48,405
From September 1st, U.S.

145
00:05:48,425 --> 00:05:50,418
Navy Lieutenant Eugene Clark's team

146
00:05:50,438 --> 00:05:52,570
also operated from the island.

147
00:05:52,658 --> 00:05:54,300
Night after night, their reports

148
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:56,588
filled in Tokyo's invasion maps,

149
00:05:56,608 --> 00:05:59,559
piece by piece, and the plan took shape.

150
00:05:59,895 --> 00:06:01,976
But on September 14th, one day

151
00:06:01,996 --> 00:06:03,934
before the landing, North Korean

152
00:06:03,954 --> 00:06:06,323
troops raided Yonghung-do.

153
00:06:06,439 --> 00:06:08,923
Lieutenant Im Byeong-rae and Petty

154
00:06:08,943 --> 00:06:11,746
Officer Hong Si-uk stayed behind

155
00:06:11,766 --> 00:06:13,793
to cover their comrades' escape.

156
00:06:13,962 --> 00:06:15,467
Refusing to be taken prisoner,

157
00:06:15,487 --> 00:06:18,019
both men met their end on that island.

158
00:06:18,352 --> 00:06:20,961
Then, around midnight on September 15th,

159
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:24,329
the dark Palmi-do lighthouse blazed to life.

160
00:06:24,474 --> 00:06:26,219
Agents had climbed the island and

161
00:06:26,239 --> 00:06:28,393
relit it, and its beam guided the

162
00:06:28,413 --> 00:06:30,293
fleet into the narrow channel.

163
00:06:30,379 --> 00:06:33,616
Now, the hour of no return was approaching.

164
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,350
At dawn on September 15th,

165
00:06:36,370 --> 00:06:39,183
the fleet's guns opened up on Wolmi-do.

166
00:06:39,291 --> 00:06:41,946
After several days of bombing and napalm,

167
00:06:41,966 --> 00:06:43,744
the little island was already a

168
00:06:43,764 --> 00:06:45,883
burning mountain of shattered rock.

169
00:06:46,052 --> 00:06:48,718
Cruisers, destroyers, and Marine Corsair

170
00:06:48,738 --> 00:06:50,869
fighters took turns pounding it.

171
00:06:51,195 --> 00:06:54,123
On the bridge of the flagship Mount McKinley

172
00:06:54,143 --> 00:06:56,513
stood the seventy-year-old MacArthur.

173
00:06:56,618 --> 00:06:58,169
Watching through his binoculars,

174
00:06:58,189 --> 00:07:00,230
he knew the first card of his great

175
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:02,680
gamble was about to be played.

176
00:07:03,027 --> 00:07:07,096
At 6:33 a.m., the 3rd Battalion, 5th Marines,

177
00:07:07,116 --> 00:07:10,192
landed on Wolmi-do's northwest shore.

178
00:07:10,270 --> 00:07:12,106
On the invasion maps, it was

179
00:07:12,126 --> 00:07:14,904
marked in green: Green Beach.

180
00:07:15,073 --> 00:07:17,148
Nine M26 Pershing tanks

181
00:07:17,168 --> 00:07:18,855
came ashore with them.

182
00:07:19,206 --> 00:07:21,941
Stunned by the bombardment, the North Korean

183
00:07:21,961 --> 00:07:24,717
positions collapsed faster than expected.

184
00:07:24,823 --> 00:07:27,170
At 6:55, just twenty-two minutes

185
00:07:27,190 --> 00:07:29,299
after landing, Marines planted the

186
00:07:29,319 --> 00:07:31,318
Stars and Stripes on the summit.

187
00:07:31,651 --> 00:07:33,960
By about eight o'clock, Wolmi-do

188
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:36,350
was completely in Marine hands.

189
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:38,375
Over a hundred North Koreans were

190
00:07:38,395 --> 00:07:41,118
killed and a hundred thirty-six captured.

191
00:07:41,138 --> 00:07:43,445
Not a single Marine was killed.

192
00:07:43,789 --> 00:07:45,666
Prisoners revealed the island had

193
00:07:45,686 --> 00:07:48,182
been held by a coastal artillery regiment

194
00:07:48,202 --> 00:07:50,389
and part of a marine regiment.

195
00:07:50,499 --> 00:07:52,960
The defenders of Inchon itself appeared

196
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:55,225
to number fewer than two thousand.

197
00:07:55,309 --> 00:07:56,734
And the enemy still wasn't even

198
00:07:56,754 --> 00:07:58,259
sure whether this was the real

199
00:07:58,279 --> 00:08:00,963
landing, or just another feint.

200
00:08:01,291 --> 00:08:03,204
But the triumph was brief.

201
00:08:03,224 --> 00:08:06,197
The sea began draining away fast.

202
00:08:06,297 --> 00:08:08,405
Between Wolmi-do and the harbor,

203
00:08:08,425 --> 00:08:11,196
an endless plain of mud appeared.

204
00:08:11,527 --> 00:08:13,742
The Marine battalion on the island was now

205
00:08:13,762 --> 00:08:17,339
cut off, alone, without the main force.

206
00:08:17,462 --> 00:08:19,161
Had the enemy rolled tanks across the

207
00:08:19,181 --> 00:08:21,561
causeway, they could have been trapped.

208
00:08:21,628 --> 00:08:23,190
And so began the long,

209
00:08:23,210 --> 00:08:25,705
long wait for the evening tide.

210
00:08:26,048 --> 00:08:26,900
In the afternoon,

211
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:28,800
the sea began rising again.

212
00:08:28,888 --> 00:08:31,873
Around 5:30 p.m., landing craft raced

213
00:08:31,893 --> 00:08:34,524
toward the Inchon shore in two waves.

214
00:08:34,648 --> 00:08:37,189
The 5th Marines at Red Beach to the north,

215
00:08:37,209 --> 00:08:40,370
the 1st Marines at Blue Beach to the south.

216
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:42,532
Red Beach was no beach at all.

217
00:08:42,552 --> 00:08:45,502
It was a stone seawall taller than a man.

218
00:08:45,523 --> 00:08:47,211
The Marines had to throw ladders

219
00:08:47,231 --> 00:08:49,669
against it from their boats and climb.

220
00:08:49,690 --> 00:08:52,811
And just beyond that wall, North Korean machine

221
00:08:52,831 --> 00:08:55,226
guns and grenades were waiting for them.

222
00:08:55,590 --> 00:08:56,929
This photograph was actually

223
00:08:56,949 --> 00:08:59,023
taken at Red Beach that day.

224
00:08:59,094 --> 00:09:00,827
The man going over the wall at the

225
00:09:00,847 --> 00:09:03,205
top of the ladder is platoon leader,

226
00:09:03,225 --> 00:09:05,970
First Lieutenant Baldomero Lopez.

227
00:09:06,139 --> 00:09:08,123
Minutes later, wounded and unable to

228
00:09:08,143 --> 00:09:10,194
throw a grenade, he smothered it

229
00:09:10,214 --> 00:09:12,411
with his own body to save his men.

230
00:09:12,762 --> 00:09:15,434
Before sunset, the Marines took Cemetery

231
00:09:15,454 --> 00:09:18,290
Hill and Observatory Hill behind the beach.

232
00:09:18,410 --> 00:09:20,758
By nightfall, a solid beachhead

233
00:09:20,778 --> 00:09:22,955
stood atop the seawall, and more

234
00:09:22,975 --> 00:09:25,981
men and guns were pouring ashore.

235
00:09:26,318 --> 00:09:29,359
South Korean Marines were also at Red Beach.

236
00:09:29,498 --> 00:09:31,436
Back to reclaim their homeland,

237
00:09:31,456 --> 00:09:33,986
they swept into Inchon's streets,

238
00:09:34,006 --> 00:09:36,466
hunting down hidden enemy troops.

239
00:09:36,590 --> 00:09:38,812
Their fight, begun on this shore,

240
00:09:38,832 --> 00:09:40,315
would carry them into the heart

241
00:09:40,335 --> 00:09:42,589
of Seoul thirteen days later.

242
00:09:42,947 --> 00:09:44,639
At Blue Beach to the south,

243
00:09:44,659 --> 00:09:46,767
the Marines met a different enemy.

244
00:09:46,856 --> 00:09:48,611
Smoke, darkness, and mud

245
00:09:48,631 --> 00:09:50,284
that gripped their ankles.

246
00:09:50,451 --> 00:09:52,400
Some boats landed in the wrong places,

247
00:09:52,420 --> 00:09:54,984
but the 1st Marines fought their way ashore.

248
00:09:55,334 --> 00:09:58,412
That evening, eight big LST landing ships

249
00:09:58,432 --> 00:10:00,859
deliberately ran aground at Red Beach.

250
00:10:00,994 --> 00:10:02,624
Knowing the ebb tide would trap

251
00:10:02,644 --> 00:10:04,604
them in the mud, they unloaded

252
00:10:04,624 --> 00:10:07,006
ammunition and gear all night.

253
00:10:07,349 --> 00:10:08,207
On the first day,

254
00:10:08,227 --> 00:10:10,551
twenty-one UN troops were killed.

255
00:10:10,698 --> 00:10:12,204
Far fewer than the planners had

256
00:10:12,224 --> 00:10:14,377
feared for such a dangerous landing.

257
00:10:14,397 --> 00:10:16,832
Almost unbelievably few.

258
00:10:17,181 --> 00:10:20,538
The next day, September 16th, ROK Marines

259
00:10:20,558 --> 00:10:23,554
cleared the last enemy troops from Inchon.

260
00:10:23,673 --> 00:10:26,066
In just one day, the five-thousand-to-one

261
00:10:26,086 --> 00:10:29,025
gamble had turned the tide of the entire war.

262
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:31,359
But the road ahead to Seoul would prove far

263
00:10:31,379 --> 00:10:33,835
rougher, and far bloodier, than the sea.

264
00:10:34,594 --> 00:10:37,708
From Inchon, two roads led to Seoul.

265
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:39,856
The 5th Marines headed north to Kimpo

266
00:10:39,876 --> 00:10:42,155
Airfield. The 1st Marines drove

267
00:10:42,175 --> 00:10:44,614
down the highway toward Yongdungpo.

268
00:10:44,669 --> 00:10:46,207
They had to reach the Han River

269
00:10:46,227 --> 00:10:47,437
before North Korea could

270
00:10:47,457 --> 00:10:49,986
reinforce the defenses of Seoul.

271
00:10:50,330 --> 00:10:52,450
Dawn, September 17th.

272
00:10:52,470 --> 00:10:54,804
A pass near Ascom City.

273
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:56,972
Out of the fog came six North Korean

274
00:10:56,992 --> 00:10:59,748
T-34 tanks, carrying infantry.

275
00:10:59,775 --> 00:11:02,157
They had no idea that Marines were lying in

276
00:11:02,177 --> 00:11:04,507
wait on the low hill just ahead of them.

277
00:11:04,849 --> 00:11:06,165
The Marines waited until the

278
00:11:06,185 --> 00:11:08,031
tanks were right on top of them.

279
00:11:08,161 --> 00:11:10,975
Then bazookas, recoilless rifles,

280
00:11:10,995 --> 00:11:12,864
and the Pershings' 90-millimeter

281
00:11:12,884 --> 00:11:15,220
guns all fired at once.

282
00:11:15,336 --> 00:11:17,297
Within minutes, all six

283
00:11:17,317 --> 00:11:19,990
T-34s were in flames.

284
00:11:20,339 --> 00:11:21,936
About two hundred North Korean

285
00:11:21,956 --> 00:11:24,360
infantry fell with them, while the

286
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:26,965
records list just one Marine wounded.

287
00:11:27,304 --> 00:11:29,301
That day, MacArthur came ashore at

288
00:11:29,321 --> 00:11:31,644
Inchon to see the front himself.

289
00:11:31,764 --> 00:11:33,383
Standing on that still-smoking

290
00:11:33,403 --> 00:11:35,511
road beside the burnt-out tanks,

291
00:11:35,531 --> 00:11:38,427
he urged a faster drive on Seoul.

292
00:11:38,778 --> 00:11:40,787
On the morning of September 18th,

293
00:11:40,807 --> 00:11:43,837
the 5th Marines took Kimpo Airfield.

294
00:11:44,062 --> 00:11:47,128
Transport planes began landing almost at once,

295
00:11:47,148 --> 00:11:50,074
opening a supply route to Seoul by air.

296
00:11:50,414 --> 00:11:52,893
The North Koreans didn't give up easily.

297
00:11:52,922 --> 00:11:54,416
Through that night and dawn,

298
00:11:54,436 --> 00:11:56,362
they launched counterattack after

299
00:11:56,382 --> 00:11:58,820
counterattack to retake the runway.

300
00:11:58,877 --> 00:12:00,951
But the Marines threw back every

301
00:12:00,971 --> 00:12:02,931
one from their foxholes, and the

302
00:12:02,951 --> 00:12:05,448
enemy fell back toward the Han.

303
00:12:05,778 --> 00:12:09,059
Meanwhile, on the highway, the 1st Marines

304
00:12:09,079 --> 00:12:11,604
were stopped in front of Yongdungpo.

305
00:12:11,649 --> 00:12:14,110
Yongdungpo was Seoul's southern gateway,

306
00:12:14,130 --> 00:12:16,398
and the enemy had made it a fortress.

307
00:12:16,507 --> 00:12:18,820
Across the stream dikes, fierce

308
00:12:18,840 --> 00:12:21,492
fighting raged for more than two days.

309
00:12:21,836 --> 00:12:24,198
On September 21st, Captain Robert

310
00:12:24,218 --> 00:12:26,463
Barrow's Able Company slipped through

311
00:12:26,483 --> 00:12:28,966
a gap into the heart of Yongdungpo.

312
00:12:29,062 --> 00:12:31,433
That night, five T-34

313
00:12:31,453 --> 00:12:33,447
tanks rolled toward them.

314
00:12:33,602 --> 00:12:35,528
Cut off, the company fought back

315
00:12:35,548 --> 00:12:36,962
with bazookas and beat off

316
00:12:36,982 --> 00:12:38,707
five assaults through the night.

317
00:12:39,041 --> 00:12:41,746
At daybreak, some two hundred North Korean

318
00:12:41,766 --> 00:12:43,816
dead lay in front of their position.

319
00:12:43,918 --> 00:12:46,641
On September 22nd, Yongdungpo

320
00:12:46,661 --> 00:12:48,939
finally fell to the Marines.

321
00:12:49,079 --> 00:12:51,757
Now only one great obstacle stood between

322
00:12:51,777 --> 00:12:55,556
the Marines and Seoul: the wide Han River.

323
00:12:55,892 --> 00:12:57,479
Most of the Han River bridges had

324
00:12:57,499 --> 00:12:59,629
been destroyed early in the war.

325
00:12:59,708 --> 00:13:01,782
The only way into the capital was straight

326
00:13:01,802 --> 00:13:04,536
across the water, under the enemy's guns.

327
00:13:04,892 --> 00:13:06,950
On the night of September 19th,

328
00:13:06,970 --> 00:13:08,616
Marine reconnaissance swimmers

329
00:13:08,636 --> 00:13:10,455
crossed first near Haengju.

330
00:13:10,475 --> 00:13:12,651
But machine guns opened up from the far

331
00:13:12,671 --> 00:13:15,396
bank, and the scouts had to pull back.

332
00:13:15,725 --> 00:13:18,578
The next morning at 6:45, the 5th Marines

333
00:13:18,598 --> 00:13:21,616
climbed into LVT amphibious tractors.

334
00:13:21,671 --> 00:13:23,757
The same vehicles that had crossed the sea

335
00:13:23,777 --> 00:13:26,909
at Inchon now charged across the river.

336
00:13:26,976 --> 00:13:28,332
During the crossing, the tractors

337
00:13:28,352 --> 00:13:30,353
took more than two hundred hits.

338
00:13:30,691 --> 00:13:33,545
The Marines stormed Hill 125 across

339
00:13:33,565 --> 00:13:36,424
the river and took it by 9:40 a.m.

340
00:13:36,534 --> 00:13:38,597
Taking that one hill cost a single

341
00:13:38,617 --> 00:13:41,147
company forty-three casualties.

342
00:13:41,215 --> 00:13:43,289
By that afternoon, a solid bridgehead

343
00:13:43,309 --> 00:13:46,124
stood firmly on the north bank of the Han.

344
00:13:46,454 --> 00:13:48,335
ROK Marine battalions crossed

345
00:13:48,355 --> 00:13:50,474
alongside the 5th Marines.

346
00:13:50,642 --> 00:13:53,799
On the 21st, the newly arrived 7th Marines

347
00:13:53,819 --> 00:13:56,609
landed at Inchon and rushed to the front.

348
00:13:56,734 --> 00:13:58,104
Day by day, the bridgehead

349
00:13:58,124 --> 00:13:59,997
north of the Han grew.

350
00:14:00,343 --> 00:14:02,109
But at Seoul's western edge,

351
00:14:02,129 --> 00:14:03,742
the enemy was waiting.

352
00:14:03,762 --> 00:14:05,961
A chain of low hills ran from

353
00:14:05,981 --> 00:14:09,501
Ansan through Yonhui to Wau-san.

354
00:14:09,596 --> 00:14:12,899
Along this ridge, North Korea dug in and built

355
00:14:12,919 --> 00:14:15,658
its last line of defense for the capital.

356
00:14:15,989 --> 00:14:17,899
At first, Seoul's defenders were

357
00:14:17,919 --> 00:14:20,448
thought to number around five thousand.

358
00:14:20,567 --> 00:14:22,232
But with the 18th Division turned

359
00:14:22,252 --> 00:14:23,598
back from the Naktong,

360
00:14:23,618 --> 00:14:25,970
they swelled to over twenty thousand.

361
00:14:26,302 --> 00:14:28,526
Ansan, the highest, stood two

362
00:14:28,546 --> 00:14:31,178
hundred ninety-six meters. Yonhui

363
00:14:31,198 --> 00:14:33,397
Ridge below it, one hundred four.

364
00:14:33,464 --> 00:14:34,901
They were not high, but they looked

365
00:14:34,921 --> 00:14:36,836
down over open rice paddies and

366
00:14:36,856 --> 00:14:39,913
streets below. A natural fortress.

367
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:42,827
From September 22nd, U.S. and South

368
00:14:42,847 --> 00:14:45,706
Korean Marines hit this ridge head-on.

369
00:14:45,841 --> 00:14:47,779
Bunkers laced with machine guns and

370
00:14:47,799 --> 00:14:50,840
mortars changed hands again and again.

371
00:14:50,910 --> 00:14:53,234
The ROK Marines' 1st Battalion,

372
00:14:53,254 --> 00:14:57,216
assigned to Hill 104, paid a heavy price.

373
00:14:57,541 --> 00:14:59,391
On September 24th alone,

374
00:14:59,411 --> 00:15:01,496
the 1st Marine Division suffered two

375
00:15:01,516 --> 00:15:03,693
hundred eighty-five casualties.

376
00:15:03,862 --> 00:15:05,867
It was the bloodiest single day

377
00:15:05,887 --> 00:15:08,177
of the entire battle for Seoul.

378
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:10,497
After three days and nights of slaughter,

379
00:15:10,517 --> 00:15:13,444
the hills along the ridge fell one by one.

380
00:15:13,503 --> 00:15:15,065
The western gate to Seoul was

381
00:15:15,085 --> 00:15:16,764
finally starting to open.

382
00:15:17,499 --> 00:15:20,388
On September 24th, the 1st Marines also

383
00:15:20,408 --> 00:15:23,437
crossed the Han into southwest Seoul.

384
00:15:23,532 --> 00:15:25,686
Before dawn the next day, the U.S.

385
00:15:25,706 --> 00:15:28,794
32nd Infantry and ROK 17th

386
00:15:28,814 --> 00:15:30,819
Regiment crossed farther east.

387
00:15:30,976 --> 00:15:34,849
And at 3 p.m., the 32nd seized Namsan,

388
00:15:34,869 --> 00:15:36,809
in the very heart of Seoul.

389
00:15:37,164 --> 00:15:39,468
That night, X Corps commander Major

390
00:15:39,488 --> 00:15:41,129
General Edward Almond announced

391
00:15:41,149 --> 00:15:43,075
that Seoul had been liberated.

392
00:15:43,239 --> 00:15:45,473
It was September 25th, exactly

393
00:15:45,493 --> 00:15:47,647
three months after the war began.

394
00:15:47,816 --> 00:15:50,369
Yet even as he spoke, gunfire still

395
00:15:50,389 --> 00:15:52,667
echoed through the streets of the city.

396
00:15:53,023 --> 00:15:55,188
North Korea had blocked every major

397
00:15:55,208 --> 00:15:57,714
street in Seoul with barricades.

398
00:15:57,841 --> 00:15:59,505
Rice bags filled with earth,

399
00:15:59,525 --> 00:16:01,735
stacked higher than a man.

400
00:16:01,776 --> 00:16:05,228
Behind them hid anti-tank guns and machine

401
00:16:05,248 --> 00:16:08,845
guns. In front, the road was mined.

402
00:16:09,175 --> 00:16:11,629
On the main boulevards, a wall like this

403
00:16:11,649 --> 00:16:14,211
appeared every two to three hundred meters.

404
00:16:14,335 --> 00:16:15,987
Each took forty-five minutes on

405
00:16:16,007 --> 00:16:18,036
average to break, as the Marines

406
00:16:18,056 --> 00:16:20,221
retook Seoul block by block.

407
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:21,560
And in the middle of those

408
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:23,550
streets were the people of Seoul.

409
00:16:23,719 --> 00:16:25,168
More than a million residents

410
00:16:25,188 --> 00:16:26,624
are thought to have been unable

411
00:16:26,644 --> 00:16:29,083
to flee three months earlier.

412
00:16:29,180 --> 00:16:31,607
Hiding in cellars and air raid shelters,

413
00:16:31,627 --> 00:16:33,110
they listened to the shells

414
00:16:33,130 --> 00:16:34,783
passing overhead and waited,

415
00:16:34,803 --> 00:16:37,229
hoping to survive until liberation.

416
00:16:37,572 --> 00:16:39,942
First, aircraft and mortars pounded

417
00:16:39,962 --> 00:16:41,969
the wall. Then tanks moved up

418
00:16:41,989 --> 00:16:44,369
to knock out the anti-tank guns.

419
00:16:44,475 --> 00:16:46,960
Engineers cleared the mines, and infantry

420
00:16:46,980 --> 00:16:49,644
advanced, searching every building.

421
00:16:49,784 --> 00:16:51,358
Snipers could be hiding behind

422
00:16:51,378 --> 00:16:53,476
any roof or window, so no one

423
00:16:53,496 --> 00:16:55,842
could relax for a single step.

424
00:16:55,993 --> 00:16:58,602
Meanwhile, street after street of the old

425
00:16:58,622 --> 00:17:01,779
capital was swallowed by fire and smoke.

426
00:17:02,095 --> 00:17:04,394
Before dawn on September 26th,

427
00:17:04,414 --> 00:17:06,305
about a thousand North Korean

428
00:17:06,325 --> 00:17:08,559
troops counterattacked Namsan.

429
00:17:08,694 --> 00:17:11,292
The 32nd Infantry held, keeping the

430
00:17:11,312 --> 00:17:13,682
high ground in the heart of the city.

431
00:17:14,025 --> 00:17:16,521
Then, at dawn on September 27th,

432
00:17:16,541 --> 00:17:18,966
the Marines finally reached the Capitol.

433
00:17:19,021 --> 00:17:21,414
The old Japanese Government-General Building,

434
00:17:21,434 --> 00:17:23,895
where South Korea's government had sat.

435
00:17:24,022 --> 00:17:26,518
ROK Marine platoon leader Park Jeong-mo

436
00:17:26,538 --> 00:17:28,600
and his men tore down the North

437
00:17:28,620 --> 00:17:31,670
Korean flag and raised the Taegukgi.

438
00:17:31,995 --> 00:17:33,740
Three months after Seoul fell,

439
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:36,664
the South Korean flag flew there once more.

440
00:17:36,723 --> 00:17:39,275
That same day, U.S. Marines raised

441
00:17:39,295 --> 00:17:40,994
the Stars and Stripes over the

442
00:17:41,014 --> 00:17:43,320
old American and Soviet embassies.

443
00:17:43,656 --> 00:17:46,121
On September 28th, the Marines took

444
00:17:46,141 --> 00:17:48,386
the last hills north of Seoul.

445
00:17:48,496 --> 00:17:51,014
Thirteen days after the Inchon landing,

446
00:17:51,034 --> 00:17:53,990
Seoul was fully back in friendly hands.

447
00:17:54,335 --> 00:17:57,172
At noon the next day, September 29th,

448
00:17:57,192 --> 00:17:58,799
a ceremony restoring the

449
00:17:58,819 --> 00:18:00,722
capital was held at the Capitol.

450
00:18:00,803 --> 00:18:02,604
MacArthur told President Syngman

451
00:18:02,624 --> 00:18:05,016
Rhee he was returning Seoul to him.

452
00:18:05,185 --> 00:18:06,770
Glass from the shell-shattered

453
00:18:06,790 --> 00:18:09,260
roof fell throughout his speech.

454
00:18:09,595 --> 00:18:11,993
With Seoul gone, the North Korean army

455
00:18:12,013 --> 00:18:14,473
on the Naktong was suddenly cut off.

456
00:18:14,573 --> 00:18:17,262
The U.S. Eighth Army and ROK forces,

457
00:18:17,282 --> 00:18:19,425
attacking from September 16th,

458
00:18:19,445 --> 00:18:22,384
drove north in just ten days.

459
00:18:22,494 --> 00:18:26,185
On September 26th, near Osan, UN forces

460
00:18:26,205 --> 00:18:28,802
from north and south finally linked up.

461
00:18:29,136 --> 00:18:31,194
Without supplies or a road home,

462
00:18:31,214 --> 00:18:33,880
North Korean troops threw down their weapons by

463
00:18:33,900 --> 00:18:36,748
the thousands and fled into the hills.

464
00:18:36,916 --> 00:18:39,605
From Inchon to Seoul, UN and South

465
00:18:39,625 --> 00:18:41,575
Korean casualties numbered

466
00:18:41,595 --> 00:18:43,816
about three thousand five hundred.

467
00:18:44,009 --> 00:18:45,890
X Corps estimated about fourteen

468
00:18:45,910 --> 00:18:47,791
thousand North Koreans killed

469
00:18:47,811 --> 00:18:50,229
and some seven thousand captured.

470
00:18:50,569 --> 00:18:52,521
One landing at Inchon completely

471
00:18:52,541 --> 00:18:54,410
reversed the course of the war.

472
00:18:54,467 --> 00:18:56,064
After three months of retreat,

473
00:18:56,084 --> 00:18:59,444
it was now the UN's turn to drive north.

474
00:18:59,497 --> 00:19:01,458
But this sweeping victory would soon

475
00:19:01,478 --> 00:19:04,349
bring a new enemy across the Yalu.

476
00:19:04,680 --> 00:19:07,141
So why did a landing that succeeded in one

477
00:19:07,161 --> 00:19:10,054
day take thirteen days to reach Seoul?

478
00:19:10,114 --> 00:19:12,743
Let's look closely at four reasons.

479
00:19:13,081 --> 00:19:16,246
First: the Han River, a giant moat.

480
00:19:16,573 --> 00:19:18,926
With the bridges down, the Marines had to

481
00:19:18,946 --> 00:19:21,497
cross the river in tractors and boats.

482
00:19:21,665 --> 00:19:23,284
Gathering enough crossing equipment

483
00:19:23,304 --> 00:19:24,776
and securing bridgeheads under

484
00:19:24,796 --> 00:19:28,212
fire ate up several precious days.

485
00:19:28,551 --> 00:19:30,628
Second: a swelling garrison.

486
00:19:30,982 --> 00:19:33,090
Hearing of the landing, North Korea

487
00:19:33,110 --> 00:19:35,253
even turned back units headed south,

488
00:19:35,273 --> 00:19:37,290
and poured troops into Seoul.

489
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:39,611
The closer the Marines came to the city,

490
00:19:39,631 --> 00:19:42,025
the more defenders stood in their way.

491
00:19:42,378 --> 00:19:44,280
Third: a double wall of

492
00:19:44,300 --> 00:19:46,375
ridges and barricades.

493
00:19:46,735 --> 00:19:49,333
Once at Ansan and Yonhui Ridge,

494
00:19:49,353 --> 00:19:52,041
and again at the barricades downtown.

495
00:19:52,147 --> 00:19:53,800
The enemy wrapped Seoul in two

496
00:19:53,820 --> 00:19:56,191
walls of defense, and demanded blood

497
00:19:56,211 --> 00:19:58,585
for every single step forward.

498
00:19:58,923 --> 00:20:01,498
Fourth: a command in a hurry.

499
00:20:01,850 --> 00:20:04,475
Almond drove his units hard, determined to

500
00:20:04,495 --> 00:20:07,650
retake Seoul on the three-month anniversary.

501
00:20:07,739 --> 00:20:09,654
He clashed sharply with 1st Marine

502
00:20:09,674 --> 00:20:11,351
Division commander General Oliver

503
00:20:11,371 --> 00:20:13,717
Smith over where to cross the Han.

504
00:20:13,832 --> 00:20:15,758
The three days between that hasty

505
00:20:15,778 --> 00:20:18,217
announcement and the real recapture

506
00:20:18,237 --> 00:20:20,930
show the true weight of this battle.

507
00:20:21,418 --> 00:20:23,261
If the single day at Inchon was

508
00:20:23,281 --> 00:20:25,435
a victory of daring surprise,

509
00:20:25,490 --> 00:20:27,667
the thirteen days in Seoul were a

510
00:20:27,687 --> 00:20:30,890
victory won block by block, in blood.

511
00:20:31,249 --> 00:20:33,407
Coming out after three months, the people

512
00:20:33,427 --> 00:20:36,570
of Seoul waved flags they had kept hidden.

513
00:20:36,611 --> 00:20:38,094
But much of the city they returned

514
00:20:38,114 --> 00:20:41,109
to was already in ruins and ashes.

515
00:20:41,166 --> 00:20:43,320
Countless people had lost their homes,

516
00:20:43,340 --> 00:20:46,043
and countless more had lost their families.

517
00:20:46,528 --> 00:20:48,548
From the fierce channels off Inchon

518
00:20:48,568 --> 00:20:51,029
to the narrow alleys of Seoul.

519
00:20:51,155 --> 00:20:54,527
In those thirteen days, countless names fell,

520
00:20:54,547 --> 00:20:56,681
many of them never to be remembered.

521
00:20:57,039 --> 00:21:00,302
Today, a lighthouse still stands on Palmi-do,

522
00:21:00,322 --> 00:21:03,199
shining over the sea off Inchon.

523
00:21:03,239 --> 00:21:05,609
We hope you'll remember, just once,

524
00:21:05,629 --> 00:21:08,792
those who risked their lives for that light.

525
00:21:09,109 --> 00:21:10,768
If this story meant something to you,

526
00:21:10,788 --> 00:21:12,941
please subscribe and hit like.

527
00:21:12,999 --> 00:21:14,276
We'll keep telling the forgotten

528
00:21:14,296 --> 00:21:16,659
stories of war in the next video.
